    {
      "id": "eecd67d9-d6b3-46f8-8e61-e5c7ad837827",
      "name": "Why MCP is Awesome",
      "path": "WWW / Blog / Why MCP is Awesome",
      "docTypeId": "a4c5fef6-cff4-4503-9eee-7a87acf5ece1",
      "templateId": "1d17992f-1fe5-4e20-8ace-975857b51b66",
      "state": "Draft",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "title",
          "value": "Why MCP is Awesome"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "summary",
          "value": "MCP transforms how AI systems interact with the world by providing a standardized protocol for connecting to data and services."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "content",
          "value": {
            "markup": "<h2>The Power of Standardization</h2><p>Model Context Protocol (MCP) is a revolutionary approach to AI integration. Instead of building custom connectors for each tool or data source, MCP provides a unified protocol that allows any LLM to interact with any compatible service.</p><h2>Key Benefits</h2><ul><li><strong>Universal Compatibility:</strong> One protocol works across multiple AI models and services</li><li><strong>Security:</strong> Standardized authentication and authorization patterns</li><li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Easily add new data sources and tools without changing your AI application</li><li><strong>Maintainability:</strong> Reduce complexity by using standard interfaces</li></ul><h2>Real-World Impact</h2><p>With MCP, developers can focus on building innovative AI applications instead of spending time on integration plumbing. This accelerates development cycles and enables more creative use of AI capabilities.</p>",
            "blocks": {
              "layout": {},
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": []
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [],
      "docType": "blogPost",
      "template": "blogPost"
    },
    {
      "id": "9631ef59-b636-4977-936d-13990913e8a9",
      "name": "Getting Started with MCP",
      "path": "WWW / Blog / Getting Started with MCP",
      "docTypeId": "a4c5fef6-cff4-4503-9eee-7a87acf5ece1",
      "templateId": "1d17992f-1fe5-4e20-8ace-975857b51b66",
      "state": "Draft",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "title",
          "value": "Getting Started with MCP"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "summary",
          "value": "Learn how to implement MCP in your projects and unlock the full potential of AI-powered data integration."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "content",
          "value": {
            "markup": "<h2>What is MCP?</h2><p>Model Context Protocol is a framework that enables AI models to interact with external systems through a standardized interface. It defines how tools, resources, and data should be structured for AI consumption.</p><h2>Getting Started Steps</h2><ol><li><strong>Understand the Spec:</strong> Familiarize yourself with MCP fundamentals and the protocol specification</li><li><strong>Choose Your Platform:</strong> Select an LLM provider that supports MCP (Claude, OpenAI, and others)</li><li><strong>Build Your First Server:</strong> Create an MCP server that exposes your data or services</li><li><strong>Connect and Test:</strong> Integrate your server with an AI application and test interactions</li><li><strong>Scale and Iterate:</strong> Expand to more data sources and refine your implementation</li></ol><h2>Best Practices</h2><p>Start small with a simple data source or service. Test thoroughly before moving to production. Use proper authentication and encryption. Monitor performance and usage patterns to optimize your implementation.</p>",
            "blocks": {
              "layout": {},
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": []
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [],
      "docType": "blogPost",
      "template": "blogPost"
    }
